  urldate = {2025-03-27},
  abstract = {The rotational spectra of HD35Cl+, H235Cl+, and H237Cl+ were measured in the frequency region of 180 to 550 GHz with a source-modulated submillimeter-wave spectrometer. The ions were generated in a free space absorption cell by a hollow-cathode discharge in a gas mixture of Cl2 and He to which H2 and/or D2 was added. Precise molecular constants including the electric quadrupole coupling constants were determined for the three species. The zero-point average structure, rz, was calculated from moments of inertia corrected for harmonic terms. The harmonic force field of H2Cl+ was derived from previously estimated harmonic vibrational frequencies and inertial defects. The rz structures obtained are rz(Cl-H)=1.320 57(48)\AA{} and \texttheta z(H-Cl-H)=94.238(72){$^\circ$} for H235Cl+ and rzCl-H)=1.320 60(38)\AA{} and \texttheta z(H-Cl-H)=94.239(58){$^\circ$} for H237Cl+, where values in parentheses indicate errors due to residual inertial defects among the corrected moments of inertia. When the \texttheta z of HD35Cl+ is assumed to be the same as that of H235Cl+, the rz(Cl-H) and rz(Cl-D) lengths of HD35Cl+ are calculated to be 1.320 69(69) \AA{} and 1.316 54(111) \AA, respectively.},
